preConditionHook:
// THE STOP ENTRY WAS ACTUALLY TRADED THROUGH, NOT GAPPED THROUGH. A buy stop
// sits entryTicks above the signal bar's high; take it only if THIS bar OPENED
// BELOW that level and traded up through it. If the market opened above the
// level the fill would be at the open, far past the intended price, and Brooks
// skips the trade (R18-06). Mirrored for shorts.
//
// EL: longStop = high[1] + entryTicks * tickSize;
//     open < longStop and high >= longStop
//
// ONE RULE, NOT TWO. The two terms are a single event -- the level was crossed
// during the bar -- and neither half is the claim: the open term alone says only
// that price was below a level, the high term alone is the ordinary breakout.
//
// Not PriorHighBreakoutWithOffset, which reads the *closing price* against that level
// and has no cap on the open; the cap on the open is the whole content here.
// Nothing in the corpus tests the open against the prior bar's extreme.
//
// General entry hygiene, not specific to the wedge chapter it was mined from:
// it applies to any signal-bar stop entry, and the entry pane ANDs it in.
//
// The tick is written division-first, mirroring EL's MinMove / PriceScale, and
// read once per bar into a local. Both stop levels are localVariables entries,
// never hook-scope declarations, so the hooks survive a double placement.
// Deepest bar touched: 1.
tickSize = ctx.MinMove() / ctx.PriceScale();
longStop = high[1] + entryTicks * tickSize;
shortStop = low[1] - entryTicks * tickSize;
